WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.040
Let’s talk about the Uighurs.

00:00:04.960 --> 00:00:09.480
They’re an ethnic minority in China who say they’re victims of horrific crimes.

00:00:14.640 --> 00:00:19.160
The UN thinks a million Uighurs are being detained in camps — maybe more.

00:00:20.520 --> 00:00:25.000
A day before he left office the American secretary of state called it a genocide.

00:00:30.880 --> 00:00:38.120
China says it is putting Uighurs in camps but that they're job training centres and that the accusations of abuse are a lie.

00:00:50.280 --> 00:00:57.960
So who are the Uighurs? Why is the Chinese government putting so many people in camps? And should the world be doing more about it?

00:01:05.480 --> 00:01:09.160
So much of what’s going on with the Uighurs is hard to pin down.

00:01:09.160 --> 00:01:14.320
Journalists and researchers can’t get to them — and China says there’s nothing to see anyway.

00:01:14.320 --> 00:01:18.200
Still, there’s a lot of stuff we do know. Let’s start with the basics.

00:01:20.200 --> 00:01:24.160
The Uighurs are a Turkic ethnic group. There are more than 11 million of them.

00:01:24.160 --> 00:01:30.160
Most are Muslims, they have their own language and they live in what’s called the Xinjiang Uighur Autonomous Region.

00:01:42.720 --> 00:01:44.760
That was in the late 1800s.

00:01:44.760 --> 00:01:49.440
And here’s Xinjiang on a map — it’s huge. Three times the size of France.

00:01:49.440 --> 00:01:53.600
It’s got oil, gas and a lot of the world’s cotton comes from here.

00:01:53.600 --> 00:01:57.200
Plus a part of China’s Belt and Road Initiative is being built through it. 

00:01:57.200 --> 00:02:02.800
It’s a multi-billion-dollar network of highways and railways to connect China to Europe and beyond.

00:02:11.080 --> 00:02:12.560
And there has been trouble there.

00:02:22.960 --> 00:02:29.320
Uighurs have often protested about being oppressed. Many have called for their own state. And some have organised attacks.

00:02:32.880 --> 00:02:38.840
While China hosted the Olympics a series of bus bombings were claimed by a group called Turkistan Islamic Party.

00:02:40.920 --> 00:02:48.520
In 2009 there were riots and fighting between the Uighurs and Han Chinese and 200 people died, most of them ethnic Han.

00:02:50.760 --> 00:02:58.200
And in 2014 a bombing at a market and a knife attack at a railway station killed 30 people and injured more than 200. 

00:02:58.200 --> 00:03:01.080
That’s when China really came down hard on Xinjiang.

00:03:30.840 --> 00:03:32.440
So what are we seeing?

00:03:32.440 --> 00:03:37.520
Well, the accusation is that China’s trying to exterminate Uighur culture and their identity.

00:03:37.520 --> 00:03:46.560
And activists and Uighurs who’ve left Xinjiang say China’s doing it in all kinds of ways — forced labour, children being indoctrinated ...

00:03:51.520 --> 00:03:54.880
… women being sterilised or forced to have abortions.

00:03:58.760 --> 00:04:02.400
It’s a long list of allegations. And then there’s all those camps.

00:04:04.200 --> 00:04:06.880
We know there are hundreds of them in Xinjiang.

00:04:06.880 --> 00:04:17.000
Activists say Uighurs from all walks of life — professors, doctors, public leaders, ordinary people too — are all detained without a warrant, charge or trial.

00:04:17.000 --> 00:04:20.200
Even loyal supporters of the Communist Party haven’t been spared.

00:04:21.120 --> 00:04:25.040
Getting a look inside one of these places hasn’t been easy.

00:04:28.000 --> 00:04:31.520
Although Chinese state media have released their version of what goes on there.

00:04:56.560 --> 00:04:59.760
But these camps don’t sound like places you’d want to end up in.

00:05:09.680 --> 00:05:22.120
An Australian think tank found that Uighurs are being forced to work in factories in Xinjiang and beyond that are in the supply chains of well-known global brands — though brands like Apple and Nike have denied using forced labour.

00:05:22.120 --> 00:05:25.000
Many Uighurs also say they’ve been tortured.

00:05:25.000 --> 00:05:27.800
Some testified before a US congressional committee.

00:05:52.240 --> 00:05:54.600
The list of abuses seems endless.

00:05:54.600 --> 00:06:00.000
Even the Uighurs who’ve managed to stay out of these detention centres aren’t free, not really.

00:06:09.440 --> 00:06:15.160
There are thousands of checkpoints, security cameras everywhere. Many use facial recognition.

00:06:15.160 --> 00:06:19.760
Rights groups say security forces also put QR codes outside Uighur homes.

00:06:29.520 --> 00:06:30.880
There’s even an app.

00:06:30.880 --> 00:06:34.400
Human Rights Watch says police use it to monitor people.

00:06:44.000 --> 00:06:46.920
China, though, says it’s not doing anything wrong. 

00:06:46.920 --> 00:06:53.680
There are no human rights violations and this is all about developing Xinjiang's economy, maintaining peace and stability.

00:07:01.480 --> 00:07:05.880
China says most Uighurs have been released from re-education camps and found jobs.

00:07:05.880 --> 00:07:15.440
This report, though, found that detention camps are being expanded. It puts the number at more than 380. That’s about 40% higher than previous estimates.

00:07:15.440 --> 00:07:19.840
There was even a time when Chinese officials denied the camps existed at all.

00:07:36.720 --> 00:07:41.520
And Uighur activists who’ve left Xinjiang say China just wants to shut this story down.

00:07:41.520 --> 00:07:45.360
They accuse the government of going after family members they’ve left behind.

00:07:50.040 --> 00:07:55.640
Rushan, who we’ve been speaking to, has lived it. Her sister went missing for more than two years.

00:08:07.600 --> 00:08:12.520
Rights groups have been saying this stuff for years. There are heaps of investigations online.

00:08:12.520 --> 00:08:14.480
So what’s being done about it? 

00:08:14.480 --> 00:08:24.800
Well, at the United Nations dozens of countries have condemned what’s happening to the Uighurs but they can’t exactly pass a resolution against China because it has veto power in the Security Council.

00:08:24.800 --> 00:08:32.400
Uighurs even asked the International Criminal Court to investigate but that went nowhere because the ICC has no jurisdiction in China.

00:08:32.400 --> 00:08:35.400
Plus China has a way of throwing its weight around.

00:08:35.400 --> 00:08:41.640
Multi-billion-dollar investment projects around the world are seen as helping to keep countries onside.

00:08:57.120 --> 00:09:02.720
Still, you can’t keep everybody quiet. A UK tribunal was pretty blunt about its findings. 

00:09:08.160 --> 00:09:13.560
The US has also blacklisted Chinese officials and blocked certain imports from Xinjiang.

00:09:13.560 --> 00:09:21.720
And just before President Trump left the White House his secretary of state called what China’s doing a genocide. President Biden’s people agree.

00:09:41.840 --> 00:09:45.960
It’s one thing to call something a genocide, it’s another to prove it. 

00:09:45.960 --> 00:09:55.960
The voices demanding justice are growing louder and there are more of them all the time. That’s not something that China or the world can easily tune out.

00:09:58.760 --> 00:10:00.680
Al Jazeera’s covered this story a lot. 

00:10:00.680 --> 00:10:06.960
I recommend watching the 101 East documentary Nowhere to Call Home, which we used clips from in this episode. 

00:10:06.960 --> 00:10:09.360
You can find that on YouTube. I’ll see you next week.

